# the curriculum for `arsenal learn`, kept as DATA so the copy can
# change without touching the engine. read by ~/tools/learn.
#
# FORMAT
#   :id  title            opens a mission (id = one lowercase word, the jump name)
#   >tool  mode           opens a step teaching one tool; mode = note | run | show
#   then TAB-indented single-line fields (no multi-line values):
#     what:  in plain words, what the tool is (aim it at a nine-year-old)
#     why:   why you would ever reach for it
#     run:   the command. tokens the engine fills in:
#              @x   -> sh ~/tools/xexec            (run one carried tool)
#              @py  -> the carried python3          (run a python one-liner/script)
#              @t   -> the range's scratch folder    (safe, wiped on exit)
#              @port @url  -> the practice website (http://127.0.0.1:PORT)
#              @ap  -> the practice login page      (needs a password)
#              @w   -> a tiny practice wordlist
#              @hash-> a practice password hash
#     see:   (mode run)  what to look for in the output that just scrolled past
#     note:  (mode show) why it is not run here, and the rule that protects you
#
# modes: note = a screen to read, no command.  run = the engine runs it against the
# range's own safe practice targets on 127.0.0.1.  show = printed, never run here,
# because it needs a real target you own (there is no safe local stand-in).
#
# THE ONE RULE, repeated so it is never far away: only ever point these tools at a
# machine you own or have written permission to test. everything the range runs is
# aimed at itself, on 127.0.0.1, and cleans up after itself.

:start   what this is, and how to touch it safely
>safety  note
	what: this stick carries 42 tools. some read a file, some fix a broken disk, some knock on a computer to find open doors.
	why: the tools are sharp. pointed at a stranger's machine that is a crime; pointed at your own it is how you learn and how you defend.
	note: the whole rest of this range aims every tool at THIS computer, on the address 127.0.0.1 that only means "me". never change that address to someone else's.
>xexec   note
	what: the tools live on the encrypted part of the stick, which on purpose cannot run a program directly -- that is what keeps the stick safe.
	why: `xexec` is the doorway: it copies one tool into a tiny scratch space that CAN run, runs it, then tears that space down. nothing runnable is ever left lying around.
	note: you never type xexec by hand in this range -- the range adds it for you. outside the range: sh ~/tools/xexec <tool> [options].
>python  run
	what: python is a whole programming language on the stick -- a pocket calculator, a web server, and glue between the other tools.
	why: half of what you will ever want to automate is three lines of python.
	run: @py -c "print('hello from python -- 2 + 2 =', 2 + 2)"
	see: python did the sum and printed the line. that same python runs the practice website you are about to attack.
>abduco  run
	what: abduco keeps a program alive even if your screen goes away -- unplug, walk off, come back, it is still running.
	why: over a flaky link this is the difference between losing an hour of work and picking it right back up.
	run: abduco
	see: a list of live sessions (empty is fine). start one with: abduco -n work /bin/sh . detach without killing it with ctrl-\ , return with abduco -a work.
>dvtm    show
	what: dvtm splits one screen into several panes, so you can watch a server in one and attack it in another.
	why: no mouse, no server, no config -- panes on a terminal that has nothing else.
	run: dvtm    (then ctrl-g c = new pane, ctrl-g o = next, ctrl-g x = close)
	note: dvtm takes over the whole screen, so the range describes it instead of launching it. try it after the range: sh ~/tools/xexec dvtm .

:files   read a file nobody told you what it is
>file    run
	what: `file` looks INSIDE a file and tells you what it really is -- a picture, a program, a zip -- ignoring the name.
	why: an unknown blob off a dead drive has no useful name. this is the first question you ask of it.
	run: @x file @t/mystery.bin
	see: file named it from its contents, not its extension.
>jq      run
	what: jq reads json -- the curly-brace data almost every other tool here spits out -- and pulls the one piece you want.
	why: raw json is a wall of text; jq turns it into an answer.
	run: @x jq -r '.tools[]' @t/sample.json
	see: jq reached into the file and listed just the values under "tools".
>rg      run
	what: ripgrep (rg) searches every file in a folder for a word, fast, skipping the junk.
	why: "where does this password / string / clue appear in all this loot?" -- one command.
	run: @x rg -n secret @t/loot
	see: rg found the line, the file it is in, and its line number.
>zstd    run
	what: zstd squeezes a file small and puts it back exactly -- the modern zip. lots of disk images and firmware arrive as .zst.
	why: nothing else on the stick reads .zst; this is how you open one.
	run: @x zstd -d -c @t/note.txt.zst
	see: the original text came back out of the compressed file.
>mandoc  run
	what: mandoc turns a raw manual page (the cryptic source format) into something readable, with no other software needed.
	why: the stick carries thousands of manual pages; this is the reader for them.
	run: @x mandoc @t/demo.1
	see: the raw page rendered into a clean, formatted manual.

:disks   is this disk alive, and what is on it
>pspy    run
	what: pspy watches every program that starts on the machine -- even ones you are not allowed to see the normal way.
	why: on a machine you are defending or testing, it reveals hidden scheduled jobs, the classic way in.
	run: timeout 3 @x pspy 2>/dev/null || true
	see: for three seconds pspy listed processes as they ran, then stopped. no root needed.
>strace  run
	what: strace follows a program and prints every request it makes to the system -- every file it opens, every network call.
	why: when a program misbehaves or you don't trust it, this shows what it is ACTUALLY doing.
	run: @x strace -f -e trace=open,openat busybox true 2>&1 | head -8
	see: each line is one real system call the program made as it ran.
>ddrescue run
	what: ddrescue copies a dying disk -- it grabs everything that still reads, notes the bad spots in a log, and can resume where it stopped.
	why: a failing drive gets worse every time you touch it; ddrescue reads it once, carefully, and never gives up on a bad patch.
	run: @x ddrescue -f @t/mystery.bin @t/rescued.img @t/rescue.map 2>&1 | tail -4
	see: it copied the file as if it were a disk and wrote a map of what it recovered.
>testdisk show
	what: testdisk rebuilds a partition table -- the disk's table of contents -- when it has been wiped or corrupted, making a "dead" disk mountable again.
	why: most "the drive is gone" panics are just a lost table, and this brings it back.
	run: testdisk image.img
	note: testdisk is a full-screen menu program, so the range describes it. run it on a real image with: sh ~/tools/xexec testdisk image.img .
>photorec show
	what: photorec ignores the table entirely and carves files straight off the raw disk by recognising their shapes -- last resort recovery.
	why: when even testdisk cannot help, photorec still pulls your photos and documents back.
	run: photorec image.img
	note: also a full-screen program; described here. run on a real image with: sh ~/tools/xexec photorec image.img .
>smartctl show
	what: smartctl reads a drive's own built-in health report -- temperature, error counts, hours run -- to tell you if it is about to die.
	why: check BEFORE you trust a drive with data, or before you wipe one to reuse it.
	run: smartctl -H /dev/sda
	note: this reads real hardware, so the range does not pick a disk for you. on a live machine: sh ~/tools/xexec smartctl -H /dev/sda .

:web     look at a website that is only yours
>httpx   run
	what: httpx knocks on a web address and reports back: is anything there, what is its title, what is it built with.
	why: given a pile of addresses, this is how you find the handful that actually answer.
	run: @x httpx -u @url -title -sc -silent
	see: httpx reached the practice website the range started for you and reported its status and title.
>nmap    run
	what: nmap scans a machine for open doors (ports) and asks each one what it is -- a web server, ssh, a database.
	why: you cannot test what you cannot see; nmap draws the map first.
	run: @x nmap -sV -p @port 127.0.0.1
	see: nmap found the practice website's port open and named the service behind it.
>gobuster run
	what: gobuster guesses hidden pages on a website by trying a list of common names very fast.
	why: the interesting pages -- /admin, /backup -- are never linked. you have to guess them, quickly.
	run: @x gobuster dir -q -u @url -w @w
	see: gobuster tried each name and found the hidden /secret page the range planted.
>ffuf    run
	what: ffuf does the same guessing as gobuster but is a swiss-army fuzzer -- it can hammer pages, form fields, or hostnames.
	why: when you need to try thousands of guesses in one spot, ffuf is the workhorse.
	run: @x ffuf -s -u @url/FUZZ -w @w
	see: ffuf put each word from the list where FUZZ was and reported which ones the site answered.
>nuclei  run
	what: nuclei checks a website against a library of known-problem templates -- misconfigurations and famous bugs.
	why: instead of you remembering every known flaw, nuclei tests for them all automatically.
	run: @x nuclei -u @url -silent 2>/dev/null || true
	see: nuclei probed the practice site (a plain site, so likely nothing flagged -- against a real app it lists findings).
>links   run
	what: links is a web browser with no pictures -- just the text of a page, in the terminal.
	why: to read a page a scanner found, or browse offline knowledge, without a graphical desktop.
	run: @x links -dump @url
	see: links fetched the practice page and printed it as plain readable text.

:passwords  break into a login you set up yourself
>hydra   run
	what: hydra tries a login over and over with different passwords until one works -- against ssh, websites, and more.
	why: it proves whether a real password is weak. here it attacks a login the range created, on 127.0.0.1, with a made-up password.
	run: @x hydra -l demo -P @w -s @ap 127.0.0.1 http-get / 2>&1 | grep -iE 'login:|password:' || true
	see: hydra tried each word against the practice login and announced the one that worked (the throwaway demo password).
>john    run
	what: john takes a scrambled password (a "hash") that you already have and works out the original word by trying many.
	why: hydra attacks a live login; john attacks stolen hashes offline, on your own machine, as fast as it can.
	run: @x john --format=raw-md5 --wordlist=@w @hash 2>&1 | grep -iE 'demo|cracked|password' || @x john --show --format=raw-md5 @hash
	see: john read the practice hash, matched it to a word in the list, and revealed it.

:apart   take an unknown thing apart
>binwalk run
	what: binwalk scans a firmware or disk blob for things hidden inside it -- smaller filesystems, keys, compressed lumps.
	why: a router's firmware is a nesting doll; binwalk finds every layer packed inside.
	run: @x binwalk @t/mystery.bin
	see: binwalk listed what it recognised inside the blob and where each piece starts.
>radare2 run
	what: radare2 opens a program and shows what it is made of -- its pieces, its text, how it was built -- without running it.
	why: to understand a suspicious binary safely, you read it; you do not execute it.
	run: @x rabin2 -I @t/hello
	see: rabin2 (part of radare2) reported the program's type, and how it was compiled -- all without running it.
>cc      run
	what: cc is a C compiler ON the stick: you write a tiny program and it turns your text into a real running program.
	why: build a quick tool or proof-of-concept in the field, from nothing but the stick.
	run: @x -t @T/cc cc @t/hello.c -o @t/hello.out && @t/hello.out
	see: your three lines of C became a program, and then it ran and greeted you. you just made software on a locked-down stick.

:loot    keep it, hide it, move it
>minisign run
	what: minisign puts an unforgeable signature on a file, so anyone with your public key can prove it is really yours and unchanged.
	why: before you trust a downloaded file, you check its signature. this is how you make and check one.
	run: @x minisign -G -W -p @t/key.pub -s @t/key.sec >/dev/null 2>&1 && @x minisign -S -s @t/key.sec -m @t/note.txt >/dev/null 2>&1 && @x minisign -V -p @t/key.pub -m @t/note.txt
	see: the range made a key pair, signed a file, then verified it -- "Signature and comment signature verified" means it is genuine and untouched.
>age     show
	what: age locks a file so only the right person (or the right password) can open it -- modern, simple encryption.
	why: to carry loot or secrets safely; if the stick is lost, the locked files are useless to a finder.
	run: age -e -p -o loot.age loot.tar      (lock)      age -d -o loot.tar loot.age   (unlock)
	note: age asks you to type a passphrase, which a guided range cannot do for you. try it after the range on a file of your own.
>rsync   run
	what: rsync copies a folder to another place and, next time, only sends what changed -- and can pick up where it left off.
	why: backups and moving loot over a slow or dropping link without starting over each time.
	run: @x rsync -a @t/loot/ @t/backup/ && @x rsync -an --delete @t/loot/ @t/backup/ && echo "second run copied nothing -- already in sync"
	see: the first rsync copied the folder; the second found nothing to do, which is the whole point.
>tcpdump run
	what: tcpdump watches the actual network traffic going past and can save it to a file to study later.
	why: to see what a machine is really saying on the wire -- debugging, or catching something suspicious.
	run: ( ping -c 3 127.0.0.1 >/dev/null 2>&1 & ) ; timeout 4 @x tcpdump -c 3 -n -i lo 2>&1 | tail -5 || true
	see: the range sent three pings to itself; tcpdump captured them and printed the packets it saw on the loopback interface.

:pro     the professional tools (read-only range)
>sqlmap  show
	what: sqlmap automatically finds and exploits a database bug (sql injection) in a website, and can pull the whole database out.
	why: it is the standard tool for one of the most common serious web flaws.
	run: @py ~/tools/sqlmap/sqlmap.py -u 'http://TARGET/item?id=1' --batch --dbs
	note: it needs a real, deliberately-vulnerable web app to act on -- there is no safe stand-in on the stick. only ever run it against a target you own.
>impacket show
	what: impacket is 70 tools for attacking Windows networks -- dumping password hashes, relaying logins, running commands on remote machines.
	why: it is the core kit for testing a Windows/Active-Directory network.
	run: @py ~/tools/impacket/secretsdump.py DOMAIN/user:pass@HOST
	note: it needs a live Windows domain to act on, which the stick cannot pretend to be. run only against a network you are authorised to test.
>chisel  show
	what: chisel drills a tunnel through a firewall, so you can reach machines that are hidden behind the one you got into.
	why: the first machine you reach is rarely the goal; chisel lets you pivot deeper.
	run: chisel server -p 8080 --reverse   (your box)      chisel client YOU:8080 R:socks   (the foothold)
	note: a tunnel needs two real machines -- an operator box and a foothold. no safe local demo.
>socat   show
	what: socat connects any two things -- two network ports, a port and a program, and can wrap a plain connection in encryption.
	why: the duct tape of networking: forward a port, relay a service, pivot.
	run: socat TCP-LISTEN:9000,fork TCP:INTERNAL:445
	note: pointed at a real internal host, so it is described, not run. harmless to learn, dangerous aimed wrongly.
>ligolo-proxy show
	what: ligolo's operator side -- it turns a foothold into a full network route, as if the hidden network were plugged into your own.
	why: cleaner and faster than a port-at-a-time tunnel when you own the engagement.
	run: ligolo-proxy -selfcert     (on your operator box)
	note: half of a two-machine pair; needs its agent on a real foothold. described only.
>ligolo-agent show
	what: ligolo's target side -- the small piece you drop on the machine you got into, which calls back to the proxy.
	why: it is what makes the operator-side route reach the hidden network.
	run: ligolo-agent -connect YOU:11601
	note: the other half of the pair; needs a real foothold and a real operator box. described only.
>masscan show
	what: masscan scans huge ranges of the internet for open ports incredibly fast -- millions of addresses.
	why: when the target is a whole network you own, masscan finds the live ports, then you point nmap at those.
	run: masscan -p1-65535 10.0.0.0/8 --rate 10000
	note: firing at scale is only ever ok on ranges you own -- so the range does not launch it. use nmap on the website step to see a scan safely.
>subfinder show
	what: subfinder finds a company's hidden sub-addresses (mail.x, dev.x, vpn.x) by asking public records, quietly, never touching the target.
	why: the forgotten dev server is often the way in; first you have to know it exists.
	run: subfinder -d example.com -silent
	note: it queries the public internet, which the stick may not have -- and you point it at a domain you are testing. described here.
>dnsx    show
	what: dnsx is a fast phone-book (DNS) tool -- turn names into addresses, or check thousands of names at once.
	why: the plumbing under recon: resolve, validate, and sort a big list of names quickly.
	run: dnsx -l names.txt -a -resp
	note: needs a working internet name-server to answer, so there is nothing for it to do offline. described here.
>whois   show
	what: whois asks the public registry who owns a domain or address, and when it was registered.
	why: the first fact you learn about a target -- who and where it is.
	run: whois example.com
	note: it needs the internet to answer, so it is shown, not run. try it on any domain when you are online.
>mutool  show
	what: mutool reads a pdf and can turn it into plain text or html -- pulling the words out of a document.
	why: to search or extract from pdfs the stick carries, or ones you recover.
	run: mutool draw -F txt file.pdf | less
	note: it needs a pdf to read; the range ships none. run it on any pdf: sh ~/tools/xexec mutool draw -F txt file.pdf .
>frotz   show
	what: frotz plays old text-adventure games -- the kind you type "go north" into. pure fun, and tiny.
	why: because a survival stick is allowed one toy, and these stories fit in kilobytes.
	run: frotz story.z5
	note: it needs a story file, carried under XOS-KNOW/games. run one with: sh ~/tools/xexec frotz /run/media/*/XOS-KNOW/games/if/*.z5 .
>kiwix-serve show
	what: kiwix-serve hands out offline copies of huge websites -- all of wikipedia, survival guides -- from the stick, with no internet.
	why: the whole point of the knowledge on the stick: read wikipedia in a bunker.
	run: kiwix-serve --port 8080 /run/media/*/XOS-KNOW/zim/*.zim    (then: links http://localhost:8080)
	note: it needs the zim knowledge files staged on the stick. if XOS-KNOW is present, that command serves it to the links browser.
>kiwix-search show
	what: kiwix-search greps those same offline wikipedia/knowledge files straight from the command line, no server needed.
	why: a quick lookup when you don't want to start a browser.
	run: kiwix-search ARTICLE.zim 'your question'
	note: needs the zim files present. described here; run it against a real zim on the stick.
